    "turn_text": "biggest impact on me was my grandmother. I absolutely adored her. And she was just perfect in my eyes in every way. She had a very unusual life. She was the mother of six children. And during the depression, Her husband died and on his deathbed, he said to take his trucks from his building and construction company by coal with his life insurance policy and start a coal business. She said she didn't even know how to do anything but spend money. All of a sudden she's broke with six children. So she founded the Clegg Coal Company in Greensboro, North Carolina. and was extremely successful. She headed the North Carolina Council of the Arts and it was during the time where women were not supposed to work. She always was very feminine and said my husband died, so I founded my company. But she was very successful and she inspired me because looking at her, work looked like fun, work like a challenge and a way to contribute. She said she had many meetings in her home and there was one organization, the Business and Professional Women's Organization, that she had a very strong feeling for. I asked her and she said that when her husband died, it was her friends, her female friends who saved her because they would tell all their friends, you've got to buy coal, buy your coal from Christina so she can feed her six children. So I've always been a strong supporter for women's rights, women's empowerment, and women's organizations, because they are there to help women. I saw how my grandmother was helped, and I saw how she was such an important person. She was head of the Craig Cole Company. She was head of the North Carolina Council in the Arts. She was an accomplished artist, too. And the mother of six",
